    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Josh Naylor** anchoring the lineup, representing our highest confidence starts with scores of 72.92 and 66.42, respectively. **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Keider Montero (4.45 xERA), where his positional advantage and optimal order placement (+6%) drive his efficiency. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** finds himself in a prime spot against Sean Burke, capitalizing on a favorable SP skill rating to secure his spot as our premier UTIL play. In the outfield, **James Wood** (60.25) and **Seiya Suzuki** (60.04) represent the high-floor cohort; **James Wood** continues to provide immense value at the top of the order, while **Seiya Suzuki** is bolstered by a matchup against a pitcher with a \"slow delivery\" profile, creating a +2% bump for his stolen base potential\u2014a critical factor for our roster construction.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was excluding **Mickey Moniak**, who finished with a 43.79 score. While **Mickey Moniak** benefited from a projected #2 spot in the order and a wind-aided park factor (+5%), the combination of a difficult matchup against Jes\u00fas Luzardo and significant platoon regression (-15%) kept him just outside the active roster. We are also closely monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" statuses across the board; specifically, **Gabriel Moreno** (42.15) and **Austin Riley** (44.79) are currently dragging slightly due to their presumed lower-order placement. Should **Austin Riley** move up from the #6 hole, his value would immediately shift toward \"Strong\" territory.\n\nRisks today are concentrated on the elite arms our hitters face, particularly Nolan McLean (2.93 xERA) drawing **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll**. Despite the tough matchup, **Corbin Carroll** remains a start at 59.08, as his dynamic platoon edge against the right-handed McLean provides the necessary cushion to justify the risk. We are also wary of the \"Elite Hold\" catchers and pitchers suppressing stolen base opportunities for **Brice Turang**, **Zach McKinstry**, and the aforementioned **Mickey Moniak**. While **Tyler Soderstrom** sits at 57.81, his massive park factor boost (+16%) makes him a high-upside play despite the presence of Kyle Bradish, provided he remains locked into the #3 spot.",
